{"kind":"request","text":"Design: a Git read token after its key is revoked\n\nScope: from 057d3269, decided in the act this rests on. A person learns that a teammate's key was stolen and revokes it as compromised. A Git read token that the key already took stays good, for up to 24 hours on Artifacts and about one hour on GitHub. No dap command ends it.\n\nWrite a design note. Open with that scene: what the person does, what they see, and what they can truthfully be told about the repository afterward. Then answer:\n- Whether each host can revoke a token by ID, checked against the host's own documentation or a real call. The service drops the plaintext today.\n- Whether the service should keep the host's token ID, and what that costs.\n- Whether 24 hours is the right longest lifetime. Weigh a shorter limit with renewal, which goes through membership, against the long clones and agent runs that need a token to last.\n\nDone when the note is approved by the checker and landed. The note's status says that no code is built. It needs no deployment.\n\nOrder: below the current items.","body":{"conditions":"A design note on a Git read token after its key is revoked is approved by the checker and landed on main.","target_head":"8f129cdf83c36b059adb0a4fe071da9bb7916fa8","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}